                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="19" heatingNighttime="19" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="true" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

The air leakage was quite reasonable but since you have an HRV, you can lower your air leakage as much as possible without worry of creating a situation where there isnÂt enough fresh air intake.

Some locations that were noted were the following:



-Weather stripping around old windows and doors should be replaced. The door to the garage has quite a bit of leakage around the handle; this could be remedied with some low expanding spray foam under the trim, around the frame. 

-Bathroom fans. The dampers for the two bathroom fans upstairs donÂt seem to be closing, this lead to air leakage back through the fan when itÂs not operating.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 245&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The exterior walls of your house appear to be a double wall with 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation on the exterior and 2x4 strapping with 4 inches (R12) in the interior. I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R10 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 180&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>The attic of your house was assumed to have 12 inches (R40) blown cellulose insulation. I have modelled adding 4 additional inches (R12).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 4 inches (R12) blown cellulose insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 65&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;.



&lt;b&gt;There are no rebates available for ceilings that are already R35 or greater.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <windows>The windows on your first floor are all triple pane, wood frame with a coating of low-e. The windows are old but they are still good quality and wonÂt result in much savings by switching to something newer. However, newer windows might be less leaky around the weather stripping and the crank so this could lead to savings. If you do intend to switch out the windows IÂd recommend the same style youÂve installed on your second floor; 3 pane, double low-e coating, argon filled, and vinyl frame.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing a North facing window with a triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified window rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 5&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <doors>Your patio door is similar to the windows where the window itself might not have much savings by switching it out, but the patio door was leaky so this could lead to a decent savings. The recommended door type is again 3 pane, double low-e coating, argon filled, and vinyl frame rated for zone 3.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>The HRV has a return duct opening in the crawlspace, this was intended to reduce moisture in the crawlspace but it is more likely just causing moisture problems in the rest of the house. The dryer is also vented into the crawlspace, further increasing the moisture level down there. To reduce moisture in the crawlspace you could consider passive vents that you open during the warmer months or a fan controlled by a humidistat. Also the dryer should be vented outdoors.





---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>You currently have a 178 litre electric hot water tank located in the crawlspace where it is a little cooler than the living area. There are insulation jackets made for hot water tanks that add about R5 to the insulation of the tank.

&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an insulation jacket to the existing tank, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 160&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing an insulation kit from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Your current oil fired furnace is original to the house according to the serial number which means it is 29 years old. The furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE but has likely dropped in efficiency over the years. Furnaces only have a lifespan of 20-30 years before they are no longer reliable, so it might be time to think about switching it out. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 85% AFUE. If you choose a sealed combustion unit, the combustion air duct could be eliminated.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with an oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 85% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 160&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. If you choose one with sealed combustion, that will increase the savings to &lt;b&gt;$320&lt;/b&gt; per year.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>I recommend you replace 1 toilet. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



